      "text": "To solve the problem of determining how many toothpicks are necessary to create the arrangement for the 250th stage, we first need to understand the pattern of toothpick addition.\n\n### Step-by-Step Analysis:\n\n1. **Initial Counting:**\n   - In the 1st stage, we have a single drawn square, which uses 4 toothpicks.\n   - In the 2nd stage, the square is the same as the 1st stage, but we add 3 more toothpicks to the outline, resulting in \\(4 + 3 = 7\\) toothpicks.\n   - In the 3rd stage, the square is the same again, but we add 3 more toothpicks to the outline, resulting in \\(7 + 3 = 10\\) toothpicks.\n\n2. **General Pattern:**\n   - The number of toothpicks added at each stage is \\(3\\).\n   - Therefore, the number of toothpicks at each stage can be described by the formula: \n     \\[\n     T(n) = 4 + 3(n - 1)\n     \\]\n     where \\( T(n) \\) is the number of toothpicks at the \\( n \\)-th stage.\n\n3. **Simplifying the Formula:**\n   - Simplify the formula:\n     \\[\n     T(n) = 4 + 3(n - 1)\n     \\]\n     \\[\n     T(n) = 4 + 3n - 3\n     \\]\n     \\[\n     T(n) = 3n + 1\n     \\]\n\n4. **Finding the Number of Toothpicks for the 250th Stage:**\n   - Substitute \\( n = 250 \\) into the formula:\n     \\[\n     T(250) = 3(250) + 1\n     \\]\n     \\[\n     T(250) = 750 + 1\n     \\]\n     \\[\n     T(250) = 751\n     \\]\n\n### Final Answer:\nThe number of toothpicks necessary to create the arrangement for the 250th stage is \\(\\boxed{751}\\).",
